WEBVTT

intro-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.652
Imagine an app that charts your weekly screen time. A table lists your minutes for social media, video, games, and studying, and a bar chart and a pie chart show the same week. Watch the Study row change to ninety: both charts follow instantly.

intro-2
00:00:16.652 --> 00:00:34.117
Try it yourself. Pause the narration, type a new number into any row, or click a bar, and watch which views change. Model-View-Controller is a classic way to organize an app like this, and its ideas run through most user-interface frameworks you will use.

intro-3
00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:56.414
We will describe it the way every pattern is described: the context it fits, the problem it solves, its solution, and the consequences of using it. On the way you will meet the three roles in its name. The model holds the data, views display it, and controllers handle input. Choose a code language above; the design is the same in every language.

context-1
00:00:56.414 --> 00:01:12.213
First, the context. The pattern fits interactive apps whose user interface keeps changing. Interactive means people change the data while they look at it. A changing interface means new screens, charts, and layouts keep arriving.

context-2
00:01:12.213 --> 00:01:30.177
Our visualizer fits. People type into the table and expect every chart to follow at once. And next month someone asks for a line chart, a dark theme, or a phone version. The data itself, minutes per category, changes far less often than the ways we show and edit it.

problem-1
00:01:30.177 --> 00:01:42.179
Here is a first design. One class, ScreenTimeApp, stores the minutes per category. That data is the heart of the app: it would be the same on a laptop, a phone, or a watch.

problem-2
00:01:42.179 --> 00:01:54.153
The same class also handles input. When the user edits a cell, the edit handler stores the new minutes and calls redraw. So input handling now lives right next to the data.

problem-3
00:01:54.153 --> 00:02:08.603
Redraw builds every display by hand: the table rows, one hash mark per thirty minutes for the bar chart, and each category's share of the pie. Drawing code for three charts now lives in the class that holds the data.

problem-4
00:02:08.603 --> 00:02:19.511
Clicking a bar only selects it. Yet the click handler calls the same redraw, so the table and the pie chart are rebuilt too, even though nothing they show has changed.

problem-5
00:02:19.511 --> 00:02:37.848
Before we fix the design, predict the cost of a change. You must add a line chart and port the app to a phone. Which code must you edit, and why is that risky? Pause here if you want more thinking time.

problem-6
00:02:37.848 --> 00:02:54.211
Every one of those changes lands in the class that holds the data. That is the problem: user interfaces change all the time, and when the interface is tangled up with the data, every interface change risks breaking the data and spreads through the code.

problem-7
00:02:54.211 --> 00:03:10.569
So a good solution has to meet four demands at once. Show the same data in different ways. Update every display immediately. Make the interface easy to change, even while the app runs. And port the interface to a new platform without touching the data.

model_role-1
00:03:10.569 --> 00:03:31.992
Here is the fix, one step at a time. First, move the data into its own class, Dataset; it will be our model. It stores minutes per category, the way users think about them, not pixels or widgets. Displays read it through rows, input changes it through set minutes, and nothing in it mentions a table, a bar, or a pie.

model_role-2
00:03:31.992 --> 00:03:48.001
Dataset also keeps a list of the objects that depend on it. Attach adds an entry and detach removes one. Anything with an update method can be on the list: every display, and any input handler whose behavior depends on the data.

model_role-3
00:03:48.001 --> 00:04:03.494
Every method that changes the data calls notify once the change is complete. Notify calls update on everything on the list, one after another. Dataset announces that something changed; it never decides how anything is shown.

view_role-1
00:04:03.494 --> 00:04:23.591
Second, give each display its own class; these are our views. The base View class keeps a reference to the dataset. Its update method is what the dataset calls: it asks the dataset for the current rows and redraws. Notice that the announcement carries no data; each view fetches exactly what it needs.

view_role-2
00:04:23.591 --> 00:04:41.030
Each concrete view decides only how to draw. TableView prints minutes, BarChartView prints one hash mark per thirty minutes, and PieChartView computes each category's share of the total. None of them stores the data; each one recomputes its display from the dataset.

view_role-3
00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:54.464
A view can also change what it shows without touching the data. Highlight marks one category and redraws only this view. Scrolling and zooming work the same way: they change one view, not the dataset.

controller_role-1
00:04:54.464 --> 00:05:16.519
Third, give input its own classes; these are our controllers. Each one handles the input for one view. The base Controller knows its view and the dataset, and its handle event method receives each input event. The base version ignores every event, which is exactly how you make a view read-only: give it a controller that ignores input.

controller_role-2
00:05:16.519 --> 00:05:30.167
TableController interprets edits. It turns an edit into a request to change the data: set minutes on the dataset. It does not touch any display; the views will hear about the change from the dataset.

controller_role-3
00:05:30.167 --> 00:05:43.902
ChartController interprets clicks. A click on a bar becomes a request to its own view: highlight that category. The data is unchanged, so no other view redraws. Not every input changes the data.

controller_role-4
00:05:43.902 --> 00:05:58.798
Controllers have an update method too, for when their behavior depends on the data. A Clear button that works only while there is data would need it. Ours do not, so their update does nothing, and they never join the dataset's list.

collaborations-1
00:05:58.798 --> 00:06:10.687
Now watch the new classes work together on one input. The user types ninety into the Study row. That edit reaches the table's controller, which calls set minutes on the dataset.

collaborations-2
00:06:10.687 --> 00:06:25.306
The dataset stores ninety and calls notify. Which objects receive update, and what does each one do next? Predict before the diagram continues.

collaborations-3
00:06:25.306 --> 00:06:34.494
Every view on the list receives update. The table view asks the dataset for its rows and redraws. Study now shows ninety minutes.

collaborations-4
00:06:34.494 --> 00:06:53.172
The bar chart does the same and grows two more hash marks for Study, and the pie chart recomputes its shares. Then control returns to the table's controller. That is the whole loop: a controller changes the dataset, the dataset announces the change, and every view redraws from it.

collaborations-5
00:06:53.172 --> 00:07:05.170
Now click the Games bar. The chart controller asks its own view to highlight Games, and only the bar chart redraws. The first design rebuilt every display on every click.

setup-1
00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:18.009
Before any input arrives, something must connect the pieces. That set-up code lives outside the dataset, views, and controllers, here in the main program. First it creates the dataset.

setup-2
00:07:18.009 --> 00:07:25.770
Next it creates each view, gives it the dataset, and initializes it. The view adds itself to the dataset's list.

setup-3
00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:41.852
Then the view calls make controller to create its own controller. Each view class overrides that one method to choose the controller it needs, a small use of the Factory Method pattern. Finally the view draws itself, and the app starts handling input.

solution-1
00:07:41.852 --> 00:08:03.974
Now step back. Remove the screen time and the charts, and three roles remain: they are the Model-View-Controller pattern. C R C cards summarize each role's responsibilities and the roles it works with. The Model, like our Dataset, holds the data and its rules, keeps a list of who depends on it, and tells them whenever the data changes.

solution-2
00:08:03.974 --> 00:08:19.188
The View, like our table and charts, displays the data. Whenever the model reports a change, a view asks the model for the current data and redraws itself, and each view creates its own controller. One model can have many views.

solution-3
00:08:19.188 --> 00:08:35.927
The Controller, like our table and chart controllers, receives the user's input and decides what it means: a request to change the data, which goes to the model, or a request to change only what its own view shows. All input arrives through controllers.

solution-4
00:08:35.927 --> 00:08:54.805
A UML class diagram shows how the roles interact. Views and controllers point to the model, but the model points only to the Observer interface: its list of dependents. That list is the Observer pattern you already know, and it is the model's only link back to the screen.

solution-5
00:08:54.805 --> 00:09:15.844
Follow the arrows for one input. It enters at a Controller, which asks the Model to change. The Model notifies everything on its list, and each View reads the Model and redraws. A Controller can also ask its own View to change what it shows. No arrow ever points from the Model to a specific view or controller.

consequences-1
00:09:15.844 --> 00:09:37.534
Now the consequences, starting with what you gain. You can show the same data in many views at once, like our three charts. Those views stay in sync automatically, because every change goes through the model. And you can swap views and controllers, even while the app runs: give the table a controller that ignores input, and it becomes read-only.

consequences-2
00:09:37.534 --> 00:09:53.180
You also gain freedom to change the interface. A phone version needs new views and controllers, but the same model. And general-purpose views and controllers, such as tables, lists, and buttons, can be reused across many apps.

consequences-3
00:09:53.180 --> 00:10:14.732
The costs are real too. Three roles mean more classes and more wiring, which a single label or menu does not justify. A burst of changes can trigger many redraws, and views that ask the model for data again and again can be slow. A model can say which part changed, so views can skip updates that do not concern them.

consequences-4
00:10:14.732 --> 00:10:34.023
Coupling also remains. A view and its controller are made for each other, so you rarely reuse one without the other. Both call the model directly, so changing the model's interface breaks them. And because updates travel through announcements, reading the code alone rarely tells you why a view redrew.

applicability-1
00:10:34.023 --> 00:10:50.818
With those trade-offs in mind, revisit the context. Use Model-View-Controller when an interactive app shows the same data in several ways and its interface keeps changing. For a small dialog with one fixed display, simple direct calls are clearer.

applicability-2
00:10:50.818 --> 00:11:07.226
You will often see view and controller merged. Many toolkits, and the components you write in web frameworks, both draw and handle their own input. That variant still keeps several views in sync with one model, but you can no longer swap a view's controller.

applicability-3
00:11:07.226 --> 00:11:32.584
Finally, do not reduce the pattern to Observer. Three smaller patterns work together inside it: Observer between the model and its views, Strategy for the swappable controller of a view, and Composite for views nested inside other views. And server-side web MVC only borrows the name: a controller handles a request and picks a page to render, and the model does not notify any views.

transfer-1
00:11:32.584 --> 00:11:52.718
One last design check. Your users want a line chart of the same minutes, plus a shared, read-only copy of the pie chart for a group chat. Which classes do you add, and what stays unchanged? Explain before continuing.

transfer-2
00:11:52.718 --> 00:12:12.626
Add a LineChartView with its own draw method, and let its make controller choose a controller. Create and initialize it in the set-up code, and it adds itself to the dataset's list. The shared pie chart is just another PieChartView whose controller ignores input. The dataset stays unchanged.

summary-1
00:12:12.626 --> 00:12:50.782
To finish, recall the whole pattern. Context: interactive apps whose interface keeps changing. Problem: when the interface is tangled up with the data, every interface change risks breaking the data. Solution: a model holds the data and announces changes, views redraw themselves from the model, and controllers turn input into requests. Consequences: many synchronized, swappable views and a portable interface, at the cost of more classes, extra redraws, and views and controllers that depend on the model. The summary quiz checks each part on a new example.
